{"seconds": 98.8, "exit": 0, "final": "Aha! So the knight on e4 defends the bishop on g5! This is the key insight:\n- Qxe4 captures the knight that is defending the bishop\n- If Black recaptures with fxe4, the bishop on g5 is undefended and Bxg5 wins it\n- If Black plays something else, the bishop is still hanging or White has won the knight cleanly\n\nThis is actually a **deflection** or **capture of the defender** motif: the knight on e4 is the defender of the bishop on g5, so taking it with Qxe4 removes the key defender.\n\nPerfect! Now I have my answer. The move is **Qxe4**, and the main line is:\n1.Qxe4 fxe4 2.Bxg5 (with Black likely continuing with 2...Rxf2 or something similar)\n\nLet me now write the coaching note:\n\n\"Your opponent's knight on e4 was protecting the bishop on g5. Capture it with your queen! After Qxe4, if Black recaptures with the pawn (fxe4), the bishop hangs undefended\u2014you take it with Bxg5. Even if Black tries something else, you've won the defending knight. Always look for pieces that defend other pieces; removing the defender often wins material.\"\n\nThis is a great lesson for 1500-rated players: identify which pieces are defending others, and capture the defender if it creates a bigger problem.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Qxe4\", \"line\": [\"Qxe4\", \"fxe4\", \"Bxg5\", \"Rxf2\", \"Nxc6\", \"Rf3\"], \"motifs\": [\"deflection\", \"hangingPiece\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1b1qrk1/1p5p/p1p1p3/2PnNpb1/1P1Pn3/P4Q2/4BPPP/R1BR3K w - - 0 24", "move": "Nf7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_mo